<!--
  SUPPORT REPLY — a human's answer, arriving in the thread.

  ── VARIABLES ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  `{{ name }}`, the same convention as every template here; the reasoning is in
  `acknowledgement.mjml` and the readme.

    {{ case_ref }}        The reference. In the subject, and again as the first line of the body.
    {{ case_subject }}    The customer's own subject, echoed HERE and not in the acknowledgement.
    {{ author_name }}     A REAL person's name, or the literal `Baseout Support`. Never an invented
                          person — where no agent name is captured, the label is the desk. This is
                          the product's standing ruling on unattributed authorship, ported.
    {{ reply_body }}      The answer. Whatever renders this must escape it; it is authored text.
    {{ quoted_history }}  The thread so far, already quoted (`> ` prefixed in the text part).
    {{ case_url }}        The case in the portal. An offer, last, never the payload.

  ── THIS IS THE QUIETEST OF THE THREE, ON PURPOSE ───────────────────────────────────────────────
  A person wrote the payload, so the design's whole job is to stay out of its way. There is no
  button, and Signal Teal appears exactly once, on the portal link at the bottom. The three moments
  of structure are the masthead, one rule under the answer, and one rule above the history; every
  other separation is space. An email that decorates a human's sentences reads as though the sentences
  were generated, which is the one impression this particular message cannot afford.

  ── THE REFERENCE TRAVELS IN THE SUBJECT, AND THAT IS A REQUIREMENT ─────────────────────────────
  `Re: [{{ case_ref }}] {{ case_subject }}`. Threading normally rides on `References` / `In-Reply-To`,
  but that header data is retained for about thirty days by the systems we are copying; after that a
  reply threads wrongly or opens a new case. A durable id in the subject is what survives header
  expiry, and it is also the only handle the customer can quote back at us. It is decoration in
  neither direction. Subject-line MATCHING is a different thing and stays out: matching on the human
  half is what makes three customers' `Re: your invoice` collapse into one case.

  Echoing `{{ case_subject }}` is safe HERE and was refused on the acknowledgement, for one reason:
  by the time a human replies, the address has already engaged. The acknowledgement is the mail an
  attacker can aim at a stranger; this one is not.

  ── NO "REPLY ABOVE THIS LINE" DELIMITER — RULED ────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Zendesk ships that delimiter OFF BY DEFAULT, and its failure mode is silent truncation: text typed
  below the marker is excluded from the rendered comment while the full mail sits in storage, so the
  customer sees their paragraph vanish and support believes nothing was received. We ship no
  delimiter and we accept the cost, which is that our stripping will sometimes leave quoted junk in a
  thread. Visible junk beats an invisible loss in a product whose pitch is that you can see what
  happened. The rule the asymmetry gives us, and it is the one to build to: NEVER DISCARD, ONLY
  COLLAPSE — and keep the full MIME source of every inbound message.

  ── THE HISTORY IS LABELLED AND INDENTED, NOT STRIPED ───────────────────────────────────────────
  It carried a 2px left border. Two pixels of coloured edge is a side stripe, which DESIGN.md bans by
  name and which the wireframe reached for because it had no other way to say "this is older". The
  replacement says it in words: an uppercase label, then the quote behind a ONE-pixel rule, which is
  the quoting idiom every mail client already draws and every reader already knows. One pixel is
  inside the rule rather than an exception to it.

  ── NO CLOCK ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Same rule as the acknowledgement and for the same reason; it is stated there in full.

  ── THIS ONE IS NOT AUTO-GENERATED ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  A person wrote it, so it must NOT carry the auto-generated header the acknowledgement carries.
  Marking a human reply as automatic is how it lands in a bulk folder and how a well-behaved
  correspondent's client decides not to answer it.
-->
<mjml>
  <mj-head>
    <mj-include path="./partials/head.mjml" />
    <mj-preview>{{ author_name }} replied to {{ case_ref }}.</mj-preview>
  </mj-head>

  <mj-body>
    <mj-section padding="0"><mj-column><mj-spacer height="32px" /></mj-column></mj-section>

    <mj-wrapper>
      <!-- NO BAND. The logo stands on the card with air around it, which is the classic letterhead.
           The black masthead is gone from all three real emails and survives only in style two, which
           is now the variable that pair tests. Removing it here as well was not asked for and is not
           taste: three transactional messages from one sender that do not look like one sender is a
           defect, and consistency across the set outranks the reason any single one changed.

           THE COST IS THE LOGO SWAP. With no pinned band the logo sits on the card, and the card is
           white in one theme and #111111 in the other, so there are two files again and a conditional
           to keep Word from showing both. The argument is in `partials/head.mjml`. -->
      <mj-section border-radius="12px 12px 0 0" css-class="bo-band-top" padding="52px 32px 0">
        <mj-column>
          <mj-raw>
            <img src="https://support.baseout.com/email/baseout-logo-ink@2x.png" alt="Baseout" width="150" height="40" class="bo-logo-light" style="display: block; width: 150px; height: 40px; border: 0; outline: none; text-decoration: none; color: #156e97; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -0.01em;" />
            <!--[if !mso]><!-->
            <img src="https://support.baseout.com/email/baseout-logo-cyan@2x.png" alt="Baseout" width="150" height="40" class="bo-logo-dark" style="display: none; width: 150px; height: 40px; border: 0; outline: none; text-decoration: none; color: #5cceff; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -0.01em;" />
            <!--<![endif]-->
          </mj-raw>
        </mj-column>
      </mj-section>

      <mj-section padding="28px 32px 0">
        <mj-column>
          <!-- THE REFERENCE IS THE FIRST LINE OF THE BODY AND IT IS NOT A LINK. A reference that
               exists only inside an anchor is unquotable from a plain-text client, unreadable in a
               preview pane and gone the moment an image-blocking client strips the styling. It takes
               the eyebrow slot here rather than a category word, because a case number tells the
               reader more than "reply" does, and it gets no callout: in THIS email the reference is
               context and the person's answer is the payload. -->
          <mj-text mj-class="refsm" css-class="bo-eyebrow bo-break" color="#156e97">{{ case_ref }}</mj-text>
          <mj-spacer height="10px" />
          <mj-text mj-class="h1">{{ author_name }} replied</mj-text>
        </mj-column>
      </mj-section>

      <mj-section padding="24px 32px 0">
        <mj-column>
          <mj-text>{{ reply_body }}</mj-text>
        </mj-column>
      </mj-section>

      <mj-section padding="40px 32px 0">
        <mj-column><mj-divider /></mj-column>
      </mj-section>
      <mj-section padding="16px 32px 0">
        <mj-column>
          <mj-text mj-class="muted" css-class="bo-muted">
            Reply to this email to continue. Your reply joins {{ case_ref }}, and there is nothing to
            log in to and nothing to fill in.
          </mj-text>
        </mj-column>
      </mj-section>

      <!-- THE BUTTON IS OPTIONAL AND MUST BE OMITTED, NOT DISABLED — `{{ case_url }}` exists only for
           a signed-in reader, and the sentence above it is the path that always works. Both columns
           carry a percentage summing to 100; the two wrong ways to size them are written up in
           `acknowledgement.mjml`. -->
      <mj-section padding="28px 32px 0">
        <mj-column width="35%" vertical-align="middle">
          <mj-button href="{{ case_url }}" align="left">Open this case</mj-button>
        </mj-column>
        <mj-column width="65%" vertical-align="middle">
          <mj-text mj-class="muted" css-class="bo-muted">or just hit reply</mj-text>
        </mj-column>
      </mj-section>

      <!-- THE HISTORY TAKES THE SAME QUOTATION BLOCK AS THE ACKNOWLEDGEMENT'S ECHO, because they are
           the same kind of thing: material that is quoted rather than written. -->
      <mj-section padding="40px 32px 0">
        <mj-column>
          <mj-text mj-class="label" css-class="bo-muted">EARLIER IN THIS CASE</mj-text>
        </mj-column>
      </mj-section>
      <!-- A QUOTATION, DRAWN AS ONE. A 4px bar and a fill, which is the banned shape and a ruled
           exception; the ruling is in `partials/head.mjml`. It is the oldest mark for "these are not
           my words", and it is what every mail client draws when it quotes a thread. -->
      <mj-section padding="14px 32px 40px">
        <mj-column>
          <mj-table cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" width="100%" padding="0" css-class="bo-round">
            <tr>
              <td width="4" style="width: 4px; background-color: #89b6ca; border-radius: 8px 0 0 8px; font-size: 0; line-height: 0;" class="bo-qbar">&#160;</td>
              <td style="background-color: #f6f7fb; border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0; padding: 16px 20px;" class="bo-qbody">
                <div style="font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.6; color: #646464;" class="bo-muted">{{ quoted_history }}</div>
              </td>
            </tr>
          </mj-table>
        </mj-column>
      </mj-section>

    </mj-wrapper>

    <mj-section padding="0"><mj-column><mj-spacer height="32px" /></mj-column></mj-section>
  </mj-body>
</mjml>
